51:Widespread focal demyelinating lesions in the myelin-rich white matter (WM) are one of the pathologic hallmarks of multiple sclerosis (MS). However, it is now well-established that the gray matter (GM) is not spared by the disease, even during the earliest phases. 1 Early GM tissue loss (i.e., atrophy), measured using MRI, has been associated with long-term accumulation of disability and cognitive impairment in patients with MS. 2 Studying the dynamics of GM atrophy development during the /uniFB01 rst stages of the disease may therefore provide important clues about the factors responsible for long-term clinical outcomes. The most suitable patient groups for such studies are those who present with clinically isolated syndromes and pediatric patients.
53:Given the rarity of pediatric MS, MRI studies of this population are relatively scarce. Cross-sectional investigations have demonstrated reduced volumes of the thalamus 3,4 and whole brain 5 in pediatric patients with MS compared to ageand sex-matched healthy controls (HC). During childhood and adolescence, subtle modi /uniFB01 cations of brain structures related to maturational phenomena occurring in the brain WM and GM (myelination and synaptic pruning) have been demonstrated using volumetric MRI measurements in healthy youths. 6 Thus it is possible that disease onset during such a critical period of life might a /uniFB00 ect the physiologic trajectories of maturation. In line with this hypothesis, a 2-year longitudinal study 7 showed that disease onset during childhood leads to a failure of age-expected primary brain growth and subsequent brain atrophy.
55:Several studies in both adult and pediatric patients with MS have consistently shown regional variations in atrophy development, with the early involvement of deep GM nuclei, followed by several cortical regions located in the frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes. 8 -10 Whether this regional pattern is partially due to a failure of region-speci /uniFB01 c age-related brain growth in pediatric patients has not yet been investigated.
