55:Several studies in both adult and pediatric patients with MS have consistently shown regional variations in atrophy development, with the early involvement of deep GM nuclei, followed by several cortical regions located in the frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes. 8 -10 Whether this regional pattern is partially due to a failure of region-speci /uniFB01 c age-related brain growth in pediatric patients has not yet been investigated.
57:Starting from these considerations, this longitudinal study investigates the complex interplay between brain growth and disease-related pathology on the regional trajectories of GM volume modi /uniFB01 cations in pediatric patients with MS. To explore the in /uniFB02 uence of WM in /uniFB02 ammation on our /uniFB01 ndings, the relationship between GM volume and focal T 2-hyperintense WM lesions was assessed. To determine the clinical relevance of GM volume changes, their correlation with clinical disability and IQ 11 was studied.
67:Sixty-eight right-handed pediatric patients with relapsingremitting MS (RRMS) 12 were enrolled and underwent baseline clinical and MRI evaluation. Thirty of them were re-assessed with clinical and MRI evaluation after a median follow-up of 3.5 years (range 1.2 -6.3 years). Twenty-eight patients or the baseline cohort refused the follow-up evaluation, while the remaining 10 had the baseline evaluation performed only recently (less than 1 year of follow-up).
69:For all evaluations, patients with MS had to be relapse- and steroid-free for at least 1 month prior to clinical and MRI assessment. In addition, they had to be on stable treatment for MS for at least 6 months.
73:To overcome the di /uniFB03 culty of obtaining longitudinal MRI scans from pediatric HC, 2 groups of pediatric HC were selected for comparison of MRI volumes: (1) a group of 26 sexand age-matched HC recruited at Hospital San Ra /uniFB00 aele (HSR) with no history of neurologic dysfunction and a normal neurologic examination, studied on the same MRI scanner as patients with MS at baseline; and (2) a group of 317 pediatric HC from the NIH-funded MRI Study of Normal Brain Development (NIH HC) with longitudinal MRI assessments for brain volume quanti /uniFB01 cation (median follow-up 3.6 years, range
75:0.9 -5.4 years). 14 The data from the NIH-funded MRI Study of Normal Brain Development were downloaded in June 2016. MRI data from all available HC were initially downloaded, then an image quality check for motion and other artifacts was performed. From the /uniFB01 nal 317 NIH HC dataset considered suitable for the analysis, a subset was followed up once (n = 163; median follow-up 2.0 years, range 0.9 -4.3 years) and another twice (n = 154; median follow-up 3.9 years, range 2.0 -5.4 years) aged from 4.2 years at the /uniFB01 rst MRI scan up to 22.3 years at the last MRI scan. All study participants were Caucasian.
79:All patients underwent a neurologic examination with rating on the Expanded Disability Status Scale (EDSS) at baseline and follow-up. At baseline, patients also underwent an assessment of premorbid IQ through the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children. 15
83:Scans from pediatric patients with MS and pediatric HC acquired at HSR were obtained using a 3.0T scanner (Intera, Philips Medical Systems, Best, the Netherlands) under a program of regular maintenance (no major scanner hardware or software upgrades occurred during the study) at baseline and follow-up. The following brain MRI were acquired: (1) dualecho (DE) turbo spin-echo (TSE) (repetition time [TR]/echo time [TE] = 2,599/16,80 ms, echo train length [ETL] = 6, /uniFB02 ip angle = 90°, matrix size = 256 × 256, /uniFB01 eld of view [FOV] = 240 × 240 mm 2 , 44 axial 3-mm-thick slices) and (2) 3D T1-weighted fast /uniFB01 eld echo (TR/TE = 25/4.6 ms, /uniFB02 ip angle = 30°, matrix size = 256 × 256, FOV = 230 × 230 mm 2 , 220 contiguous, axial slices with voxel size = 0.89 × 0.89 × 0.8 mm 3 ). For all scans, the slices were positioned to run parallel to a line that joins the most inferoanterior and infero-posterior parts of the corpus callosum, with careful repositioning at follow-up.
89:A single experienced observer, unaware of to whom the scans belonged, performed MRI analysis. T2-hyperintense and T1-hypointense lesion volumes (LV) were measured on the DE and 3D T1-weighted scans, respectively, using a local thresholding segmentation technique (Jim 6, Xinapse Systems, Colchester, UK). The 3D T1-weighted images were coregistered and resliced to match the T2-weighted scans before identi /uniFB01 cation and segmentation of the T1-hypointense lesions and underwent lesion re /uniFB01 lling before GM volumetric analysis. 16
93:Regional GM growth modeling in HC and the evaluation of deviations from normative data in patients with MS required the alignment of participant images to Montreal Neurological Institute (MNI) standard space. Taking into account the longitudinal consistency of MRI scans belonging to the same participant, within-subject serial longitudinal registration as implemented in SPM 12 was used to align the available 3D T1weighted images (either 2 or 3). 17 For each participant, this step creates an image of the Jacobian determinants of the transformation between each timepoint and the midpoint average template. Then, midpoint average templates and baseline scans (for participants who had only a single scan available) were used for groupwise alignment: /uniFB01 rst, they were segmented into di /uniFB00 erent tissue types via the segmentation routine. 18 Then, GM and WM segmented images of all participants, in the closest possible rigid-body alignment with each other, were used to produce initial GM and WM templates and to drive the deformation to the templates. At each iteration, the deformations, calculated using the di /uniFB00 eomorphic anatomical registration using exponentiated lie algebra (DARTEL) registration method, 19 were applied to GM and WM, with an increasingly good alignment of participant morphology, to produce templates. Finally, an a /uniFB03 ne transformation that maps from the population average template to MNI space was calculated. With the aim of transforming each timepoint of each participant into standard space, the midpoint average templates were intensity modulated using the Jacobian determinant (obtained by applying serial longitudinal registration), and then each group of timepoints, including baseline scans, was spatially normalized (DARTEL registration), modulated, and smoothed with a 4-mm Gaussian kernel using the DARTEL tool ' Normalize to MNI space. ' The result of this preprocessing procedure was a modulated, smoothed, and normalized GM image for each participant at each timepoint; these were subsequently used to estimate regional GM volume developmental trajectories in HC and evaluate potential deviations in patients with MS.
97:Between-group comparisons of demographic, clinical, and structural MRI measures at baseline and their changes over time were performed using the Pearson χ 2 test, MannWhitney U test, 2-sample t test, and linear mixed e /uniFB00 ects models (for longitudinal data) as appropriate. T2 and T1 LVs underwent logarithmic transformation to reduce skewness.
